00:00:16.836 --> 00:00:34.713
Ch 11 (partitioning), Ch 14 (queues), Ch 39 (rate limiting), Ch 50 (scheduling), What you will build: a crawler that keeps our view of public competitor prices, product availability and catalogue changes fresh, politely, legally and within a budget.

9
00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:38.449
This is the classic design a web crawler interview question.

10
00:00:38.729 --> 00:00:46.111
We answer it for a job with sharper requirements than a search engine's: every product matters, and prices go stale in hours.

11
00:00:46.391 --> 00:00:47.457
Lab results:

12
00:00:47.737 --> 00:00:49.060
L1, the frontier.

13
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:56.892
A real HTTP server simulated 100 sites, each allowing one request every 500 ms:

14
00:00:58.242 --> 00:01:08.845
A global FIFO that fetched immediately got 307 HTTP 429 responses, was banned by 4 sites, and fetched 49 pages.

15
00:01:09.125 --> 00:01:13.263
Waiting politely in place fetched only 625 pages.

16
00:01:13.543 --> 00:01:23.314
Per-host queues with a ready-host scheduler fetched 3,826 of a 4,000-page ceiling, with zero 429s.

17
00:01:23.594 --> 00:01:24.661
L2, dedup.

18
00:01:24.941 --> 00:01:27.735
12M links pointed to 2M real pages:

19
00:01:28.015 --> 00:01:33.114
Without canonicalisation the crawler made 8.77M duplicate fetches.

20
00:01:34.464 --> 00:01:44.021
A set of canonical 64-bit hashes gave exact dedup in 64 MB, against 1.47 GB for raw URL strings.

21
00:01:44.301 --> 00:01:51.299
A Bloom filter used 2.3 MB, but silently never fetched 3,380 real pages.

22
00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:53.477
L3, re-crawl scheduling.

23
00:01:53.757 --> 00:01:58.557
A whole-page hash learns nothing, because every page looks changed on every fetch.

24
00:01:58.837 --> 00:02:08.736
Price-based proportional allocation lifted flash-deal freshness from 34% to 86%, but lowered overall freshness below uniform.

25
00:02:10.086 --> 00:02:14.246
Optimal allocation beats uniform only with good rate estimates:

26
00:02:14.526 --> 00:02:20.563
94.9% with true rates against 92.5% for uniform.

27
00:02:20.843 --> 00:02:21.995
L4, traps.

28
00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:42.691
Breadth-first crawling spent 17,449 of 20,000 fetches on facets, calendars and session-id duplicates, and found 2,823 of 5,000 products. robots.txt plus sitemap.xml found all 5,000 products in 5,301 fetches.

29
00:02:42.971 --> 00:02:43.953
