30
00:02:44.233 --> 00:02:45.854
100 min + code.

31
00:02:47.204 --> 00:02:47.951
Warning:

32
00:02:48.231 --> 00:02:51.260
Legal and ethical scope (VERIFY with counsel).

33
00:02:51.540 --> 00:03:05.471
Collecting competitors' public prices is common, but terms of service, copyright and database rights, anti-circumvention and computer-misuse laws, and data-protection rules vary by jurisdiction and change over time.

34
00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:17.996
This chapter assumes: public pages only;. robots.txt honoured;. identified user agents;. conservative rate limits;. no login, CAPTCHA or anti-bot circumvention;.

35
00:03:19.346 --> 00:03:20.626
no personal data.

36
00:03:20.906 --> 00:03:25.450
Where available, prefer official feeds, affiliate APIs or partnerships.

37
00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:27.565
Nothing here is legal advice.

38
00:03:28.915 --> 00:03:29.768
Part 1.

39
00:03:30.048 --> 00:03:31.968
Introduction and Why It Matters.

40
00:03:33.218 --> 00:03:35.543
A story: the crawler that got us blocked.

41
00:03:35.823 --> 00:03:39.429
The first price monitor was a Python script on a cron job.

42
00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:50.759
For each of 40,000 tracked products on six competitor sites, it fetched the page every hour with 50 threads, parsed the price with a CSS selector, and wrote it to a table.

43
00:03:51.039 --> 00:03:52.362
Four things went wrong:

44
00:03:52.642 --> 00:03:55.586
Two sites blocked the office IP range within a week.

45
00:03:55.866 --> 00:03:58.639
Fifty threads had been hitting the same site at once.

46
00:03:58.919 --> 00:04:04.253
The platform's own marketing team, on the same IPs, lost access to those sites too.

47
00:04:05.603 --> 00:04:07.331
Half the fetches were duplicates.

48
00:04:07.611 --> 00:04:09.915
Links carried utm and session parameters.

49
00:04:10.195 --> 00:04:15.123
The same product was fetched under six spellings, and each spelling had a different last price.

50
00:04:15.403 --> 00:04:17.195
Prices were stale where it mattered.

51
00:04:17.475 --> 00:04:21.037
Flash deals changed hourly and were checked hourly at best.

52
00:04:21.317 --> 00:04:26.309
Staple products that changed monthly were also checked hourly, using up the budget.

53
00:04:26.589 --> 00:04:28.360
A redesign broke everything.

54
00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.795
One site redesigned its product page.

55
00:04:31.075 --> 00:04:40.184
The selector returned nothing, the pipeline stored null prices as 0, and the pricing engine (Ch 49) nearly matched 0 rupees.

56
00:04:41.534 --> 00:04:59.155
A crawler is a distributed scheduler with manners: a frontier that respects every site's limits, dedup at URL and content level, re-crawl policies that spend a budget where freshness pays, robust extraction with validation, and trap avoidance, all under legal constraints.

57
00:05:00.505 --> 00:05:01.188
Quote:

58
00:05:01.468 --> 00:05:03.132
The thesis of this chapter.

59
00:05:03.412 --> 00:05:04.863
The frontier is per host.

60
00:05:05.143 --> 00:05:12.460
Separate queues per site, scheduled by when each site may next be contacted, maximise throughput and politeness (L1).

61
00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:16.367
Dedup canonical URLs with compact exact structures.

62
00:05:16.647 --> 00:05:21.297
A probabilistic structure's false positives are pages you never see (L2).

63
00:05:21.577 --> 00:05:28.553
Re-crawl on the signal you care about: extract the price, estimate its change rate, and weight by business value.

64
00:05:28.833 --> 00:05:32.097
A uniform schedule is a strong baseline (L3).

65
00:05:32.377 --> 00:05:38.713
Use what sites publish: sitemaps and robots.txt beat heuristics against traps (L4).

