167
00:10:25.959 --> 00:10:27.111
Links are skewed.

168
00:10:27.391 --> 00:10:32.041
A few sites hold most links, so a global FIFO is dominated by a few hosts.

169
00:10:32.321 --> 00:10:36.247
The Mercator design (Heydon and Najork) splits the frontier in two:

170
00:10:36.527 --> 00:10:39.065
Front queues order URLs by priority.

171
00:10:39.345 --> 00:10:41.415
Back queues hold URLs per host.

172
00:10:41.695 --> 00:10:46.665
A heap of hosts, keyed by their next-allowed time, hands workers only ready hosts.

173
00:10:48.015 --> 00:11:02.650
Politeness state per host: crawl-delay from robots.txt, adaptive backoff on 429 or 503 (honour Retry-After), concurrent-connection limits, and error-rate circuit breakers (Ch 18).

174
00:11:02.930 --> 00:11:08.007
Distribution: partition hosts across crawler nodes (consistent hashing by host).

175
00:11:08.287 --> 00:11:14.474
Each node owns its hosts' back queues and politeness state, so there is no cross-node coordination per fetch.

176
00:11:14.754 --> 00:11:16.461
DNS caching is per node.

