00:15:05.815 --> 00:15:08.653
Change detection must use the field you care about.

271
00:15:08.933 --> 00:15:15.717
Whole-page hashes change on every fetch (timestamps, counters, ads), so learning from them is learning nothing.

272
00:15:15.997 --> 00:15:19.346
Estimating change rates from few observations is noisy.

273
00:15:19.626 --> 00:15:26.879
Cho and Garcia-Molina's estimator corrects for missed changes, but three days can't tell weekly pages from monthly ones.

274
00:15:27.159 --> 00:15:32.045
Proportional allocation chases fast-changing pages, and starves the rest.

275
00:15:33.395 --> 00:15:41.779
The freshness-optimal allocation (equalising marginal freshness per fetch) is the right objective, but only with good estimates.

276
00:15:42.059 --> 00:15:51.637
Keep estimating continuously, use priors from similar pages (category, site), and weight by business value (top sellers, flash deals) explicitly.

