Pricing against the wrong product.

672
00:41:38.091 --> 00:41:38.966
Part 8.

673
00:41:39.246 --> 00:41:40.590
Interview Q and A.

674
00:41:41.840 --> 00:41:42.672
Beginner.

675
00:41:42.952 --> 00:41:43.976
Question 1.

676
00:41:44.256 --> 00:41:46.496
Design a web crawler, main components?

677
00:41:46.776 --> 00:41:48.141
Thirty-second answer:

678
00:41:48.421 --> 00:42:09.925
Seed URLs and sitemaps; a frontier (priority + per-host queues with politeness); fetchers with DNS caching and robots.txt handling; parsers and link extraction; URL canonicalisation and dedup; content storage and extraction; re-crawl scheduler; monitoring; all partitioned by host across nodes.

679
00:42:11.275 --> 00:42:12.278
Question 2.

680
00:42:12.558 --> 00:42:14.265
How do you make a crawler polite?

681
00:42:14.545 --> 00:42:15.910
Thirty-second answer:

682
00:42:16.190 --> 00:42:36.179
Per-host queues and rate limits (crawl-delay, conservative defaults), a scheduler that only hands out hosts whose next-allowed time has come, backoff on 429 or 503 with Retry-After, connection limits, robots.txt compliance, an identifying user agent, and circuit breakers.

683
00:42:37.529 --> 00:42:38.575
Question 3.

684
00:42:38.855 --> 00:42:40.625
Why canonicalise URLs?

685
00:42:40.905 --> 00:42:42.271
Thirty-second answer:

686
00:42:42.551 --> 00:42:55.223
The same page appears under many spellings (tracking params, session ids, case, ports, fragments, parameter order); without canonicalisation you fetch it repeatedly and store conflicting data.

687
00:42:56.573 --> 00:42:57.639
Question 4.

688
00:42:57.919 --> 00:43:01.311
What is robots.txt and what does it mean for design?

689
00:43:01.591 --> 00:43:02.957
Thirty-second answer:

690
00:43:03.237 --> 00:43:10.575
A per-site file listing paths crawlers may not fetch (by user agent), sometimes a crawl-delay and sitemap locations.

691
00:43:10.855 --> 00:43:17.618
Fetch and cache it per host, check every URL against it, re-fetch periodically, and treat it as a hard rule.

692
00:43:18.968 --> 00:43:19.971
Intermediate.

693
00:43:20.251 --> 00:43:21.360
Question 5.

694
00:43:21.640 --> 00:43:24.477
How do you know if you've seen a URL before, at scale?

695
00:43:24.757 --> 00:43:26.123
Thirty-second answer:

696
00:43:26.403 --> 00:43:43.917
Canonicalise, then store 64-bit fingerprints in a partitioned hash set (exact for practical purposes, ~8 B each); Bloom filters are smaller but their false positives are URLs never fetched, fine for web-scale discovery, wrong for must-see pages.

697
00:43:45.267 --> 00:43:46.377
Question 6.

698
00:43:46.657 --> 00:43:48.662
How do you detect that a page changed?

699
00:43:48.942 --> 00:43:50.307
Thirty-second answer:

700
00:43:50.587 --> 00:44:06.523
Conditional GETs (ETag or Last-Modified), sitemap lastmod, and hashing the extracted fields you care about, not the whole HTML, which changes every visit due to timestamps and ads; near-duplicate detection with SimHash for content.

701
00:44:07.873 --> 00:44:08.961
Question 7.

702
00:44:09.241 --> 00:44:10.948
How do you avoid crawler traps?

703
00:44:11.228 --> 00:44:12.593
Thirty-second answer:

704
00:44:12.873 --> 00:44:28.383
Canonicalisation, URL-template detection with budgets that continue only while templates yield new content, per-host page budgets, soft-404 detection, robots.txt, sitemap seeding; depth limits alone are weak.

705
00:44:29.733 --> 00:44:30.757
Question 8.

706
00:44:31.037 --> 00:44:33.234
How do you extract prices robustly?

707
00:44:33.514 --> 00:44:34.879
Thirty-second answer:

708
00:44:35.159 --> 00:44:51.245
Prefer structured data (JSON-LD Product or Offer), fall back to versioned per-site selectors; validate values (range versus last price, currency, pack size); quarantine failures; monitor extraction success and canary pages per site.

709
00:44:52.595 --> 00:44:53.661
Question 9.

710
00:44:53.941 --> 00:44:55.563
How do you distribute a crawler?

711
00:44:55.843 --> 00:44:57.208
Thirty-second answer:

712
00:44:57.488 --> 00:45:12.741
Partition by host (consistent hashing) so each node owns a host's queue and politeness state; URLs discovered for other hosts are routed to their owners; shared dedup via partitioned fingerprint sets; node failures reassign hosts.

713
00:45:14.091 --> 00:45:14.859
Senior.

714
00:45:15.139 --> 00:45:16.185
Question 10.

715
00:45:16.465 --> 00:45:18.790
How should re-crawl frequency be decided?

716
00:45:19.070 --> 00:45:20.435
Thirty-second answer:

717
00:45:20.715 --> 00:45:38.379
Estimate each page's change rate for the extracted fields (correcting for missed changes), allocate the budget to maximise freshness (optimal allocation, not proportional), add floors so no page starves, weight by business value, use priors for new pages, keep re-estimating.

718
00:45:39.729 --> 00:45:40.860
Question 11.

719
00:45:41.140 --> 00:45:42.761
Design the Mercator frontier.

720
00:45:43.041 --> 00:45:44.407
Thirty-second answer:

721
00:45:44.687 --> 00:46:02.756
Front queues by priority feed back queues, one per host (bounded number, mapped to hosts); a heap of back queues keyed by next-allowed time; workers pop the earliest ready host, fetch one URL, and reinsert the host with its next time; empty back queues get refilled from front queues.

722
00:46:04.106 --> 00:46:05.194
Question 12.

723
00:46:05.474 --> 00:46:07.842
How do you handle JavaScript-heavy sites?

724
00:46:08.122 --> 00:46:09.487
Thirty-second answer:

725
00:46:09.767 --> 00:46:25.917
First look for structured data or APIs the page calls (publicly, within terms); otherwise render with headless browsers in a separate, sandboxed, rate-limited pool, caching static resources; decide per site; it's 10 to 50 times costlier.

726
00:46:27.267 --> 00:46:28.547
Question 13.

727
00:46:28.827 --> 00:46:31.536
How do you match competitor products to your SKUs?

728
00:46:31.816 --> 00:46:33.181
Thirty-second answer:

729
00:46:33.461 --> 00:46:46.837
Barcodes when present; otherwise brand + normalised name + pack size with fuzzy matching and ML similarity; confidence scores; human review queue; pricing uses only high-confidence matches.

730
00:46:48.187 --> 00:46:49.382
Question 14.

731
00:46:49.662 --> 00:46:51.326
What should you monitor on a crawler?

732
00:46:51.606 --> 00:46:52.971
Thirty-second answer:

733
00:46:53.251 --> 00:47:09.209
Per-site request rates versus limits, 429 or 403 or 503 rates, bans, extraction success and quarantine rates, canary pages, freshness per class, frontier depth, throughput, DNS errors, cost per fetch.

734
00:47:10.559 --> 00:47:11.455
Architect.

735
00:47:11.735 --> 00:47:12.929
Question 15.

736
00:47:13.209 --> 00:47:15.769
Design a price-monitoring crawler end to end.

737
00:47:16.049 --> 00:47:17.415
Thirty-second answer:

738
00:47:17.695 --> 00:47:37.791
Site configs with legal approval; sitemap or robots ingestion; Mercator frontier partitioned by host; canonical fingerprint dedup; fetchers with conditional GETs; JSON-LD-first extraction with validation and quarantine; price history and change events; product matching; re-crawl scheduling with

739
00:47:38.071 --> 00:47:42.700
change-rate estimates, floors and business weights; monitoring and kill switches.

740
00:47:44.050 --> 00:47:45.245
Question 16.

741
00:47:45.525 --> 00:47:48.597
How does a search-engine crawler differ from a focused crawler?

742
00:47:48.877 --> 00:47:50.242
Thirty-second answer:

743
00:47:50.522 --> 00:48:09.061
Search engines optimise coverage and importance over billions of pages (Bloom filters, PageRank-like priorities, massive distribution); focused crawlers target known sites and fields with completeness and freshness requirements (exact dedup, per-page schedules, extraction quality).

744
00:48:10.411 --> 00:48:11.755
Question 17.

745
00:48:12.035 --> 00:48:14.424
What are the legal and ethical guardrails?

746
00:48:14.704 --> 00:48:16.069
Thirty-second answer:

747
00:48:16.349 --> 00:48:31.261
Public pages only, robots.txt honoured, identified user agent, conservative rates, no circumvention of blocks or logins, no personal data, per-site legal review, prefer feeds or partnerships; stop when asked.

748
00:48:31.541 --> 00:48:34.293
Laws and terms vary, verify with counsel.

749
00:48:35.643 --> 00:48:36.817
Question 18.

750
00:48:37.097 --> 00:48:40.446
How do you make crawler output trustworthy for pricing decisions?

751
00:48:40.726 --> 00:48:42.091
Thirty-second answer:

752
00:48:42.371 --> 00:48:57.305
Validation and quarantine, match confidence, freshness timestamps on every price, outlier detection, human review for large moves, and the pricing engine only reacting to fresh, confident, validated data within guardrails.

753
00:48:58.655 --> 00:48:59.892
Question 19.

754
00:49:00.172 --> 00:49:02.860
How would you scale to 100 million pages?

755
00:49:03.140 --> 00:49:04.505
Thirty-second answer:

756
00:49:04.785 --> 00:49:22.236
Many fetch nodes partitioned by host; disk-backed frontier queues; fingerprint sets partitioned and on disk with Bloom front filters; async fetchers; DNS resolvers; content stored in object storage (Ch 52); extraction as a stream; scheduling sharded.

757
00:49:23.586 --> 00:49:24.695
Question 20.

758
00:49:24.975 --> 00:49:27.727
What makes a crawler a good citizen and a good system?

759
00:49:28.007 --> 00:49:29.373
Thirty-second answer:

760
00:49:29.653 --> 00:49:42.154
Measurably polite (zero violations, fast backoff), efficient (no duplicate or trap fetches), fresh where it matters, correct (validated, matched), observable, legal, and easy to stop per site.

761
00:49:43.504 --> 00:49:44.400
Part 9.

762
00:49:44.680 --> 00:49:46.088
Summary and Practice.

763
00:49:47.338 --> 00:49:48.426
Key takeaways.

764
00:49:48.706 --> 00:49:52.845
The frontier is per host, scheduled by next-allowed time (L1).

765
00:49:53.125 --> 00:49:54.938
Canonicalise and fingerprint.

766
00:49:55.218 --> 00:49:58.866
Use Bloom filters only where misses are acceptable (L2).

767
00:49:59.146 --> 00:50:04.031
Re-crawl on the extracted field, with good estimates, floors and value weights.

768
00:50:04.311 --> 00:50:06.807
Uniform is a strong baseline (L3).

769
00:50:07.087 --> 00:50:11.077
Sitemaps and robots.txt beat trap heuristics (L4).

770
00:50:11.357 --> 00:50:15.047
Validate extractions, match products carefully, and stay legal.

